<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bologna</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bologna is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, home to one of the oldest universities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. From the eleventh century its scholars studied the recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-juris-civilis">Corpus Juris Civilis</a>, the law code compiled under Justinian. The teaching of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a> at Bologna made it a channel through which the legal legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> shaped European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bologna.jpg" alt="Bologna" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fabio Ciminelli · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Torri_di_Bologna,_Bologna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
